+# LexiQuest v0.1 Complete-System Design
+
+**Date:** 2026-07-29
+
+**Target branch:** `release/v0.1-complete-system`
+
+**Historical comparison branch:** `baseline/2024-10` at commit `4837d81`
+
+## 1. Decision and objective
+
+LexiQuest v0.1 will not ship as a partial demo. Every feature that remains
+visible in the application must use real production logic, be reachable
+through the canonical navigation, handle expected failure states, and pass its
+release acceptance tests. Mock, sample, simulated, placeholder, or fabricated
+results may exist in automated tests and developer fixtures only; they must
+not be reachable in a production APK.
+
+The first controlled release targets approximately 30 invited Android users.
+The primary architecture is **on-device first**: learning, game rules, local
+analysis, camera recognition, and persistence run on the phone and synchronize
+after connectivity returns. Cloud features truthfully disclose when they
+require a connection. The initial AI provider is Gemini only, using an API key
+supplied by each user.
+
+“Complete” means all acceptance criteria and release gates in this document
+pass on the exact signed APK candidate. It does not mean mathematical
+certainty, perpetual third-party availability, or zero possible defects.
+
+## 2. Scope
+
+### 2.1 Included feature groups
+
+The production feature inventory includes:
+
+1. Account and identity:
+   - email/password registration;
+   - email verification and resend;
+   - sign-in and sign-out;
+   - anonymous guest mode;
+   - forgot-password email;
+   - reset-password deep link/action-code handling;
+   - change password from profile after recent authentication;
+   - guest-to-account upgrade without losing local learning data;
+   - profile and account settings.
+2. Vocabulary and content:
+   - personal categories and words;
+   - bulk word entry and wordbook import;
+   - CEFR selection and diagnostic test;
+   - article reading and interactive storybook;
+   - associative reading;
+   - dictionary, phrase, collocation, and phonetic exploration.
+3. Practice and assessment:
+   - standard quiz;
+   - SRS flashcards;
+   - word and sentence scramble;
+   - fill-in-the-blanks;
+   - dictation;
+   - speech-to-text;
+   - shadowing and pronunciation feedback;
+   - weakness clinic and adaptive recommendations.
+4. Progress and game systems:
+   - learning world map;
+   - boss battle and ghost/shadow duel;
+   - mastery dashboard;
+   - streaks, quests, ranks, achievements, points, and unlock rules;
+   - shop, avatar equipment, purchased items, and wallpapers.
+5. AI, audio, and camera:
+   - Gemini AI tutor and generated learning content;
+   - native device TTS;
+   - authenticated OmniVoice enhancement when its cloud service is available;
+   - smart audio playlists and background audio;
+   - real camera capture and on-device object recognition;
+   - automatic device capability detection and bounded model selection.
+6. Research and user-owned output:
+   - learning analytics;
+   - thesis charts;
+   - CSV/SPSS-style data export;
+   - PDF, glossary, Anki/dictionary, and appendix exports.
+7. Runtime:
+   - background reminders;
+   - offline persistence;
+   - retryable synchronization;
+   - build identity, runtime health, privacy controls, and diagnostics.
+
+### 2.2 Explicit non-goals for v0.1
+
+- Providers other than Gemini.
+- iOS, desktop, or web release artifacts.
+- Public self-service enrollment beyond the invited test group.
+- Competitive leaderboards whose outcome depends on untrusted client scoring.
+- Training a new large language model as a prerequisite for the Android
+  release.
+- Shipping a large local generative model that attempts to replace Gemini or
+  OmniVoice on every supported phone.
+
+These non-goals do not remove existing user-facing Android features. They
+prevent unrelated platform and provider expansion from delaying v0.1.
+
+## 3. Version and branch strategy
+
+- `baseline/2024-10` is an immutable historical reference to the earliest
+  repository state from 2024-10-15. It exists only for comparison and is not a
+  release source.
+- `release/v0.1-complete-system` is isolated from the user's existing dirty
+  working tree and is the only implementation branch for this stabilization.
+- The Android package version becomes `0.1.0+1`. Every subsequent test APK
+  increments the build number while retaining the v0.1 semantic version until
+  the release is accepted.
+- Comparison documentation will record user journeys, feature inventory,
+  architecture, screenshots, and test evidence for the historical baseline
+  and the release candidate. Historical code is never copied merely to restore
+  obsolete behavior or styling.
+- No branch created for this work uses `codex` in its name.
+
+## 4. Product architecture
+
+### 4.1 Application layers
+
+Each feature is migrated into four explicit layers:
+
+1. **Presentation:** screens, reusable widgets, navigation, and localized
+   user feedback.
+2. **Application/domain:** use cases, validation, learning algorithms, game
+   rules, and provider-independent contracts.
+3. **Local data:** Drift/SQLite repositories, cached content, pending sync
+   operations, and migration logic.
+4. **Remote adapters:** Firebase identity/data/functions, Gemini, and the
+   optional voice service.
+
+Screens do not call Firebase, HTTP clients, Supabase, or platform plugins
+directly. They invoke application services through injected interfaces. This
+keeps offline behavior deterministic and makes each production path testable.
+
+### 4.2 Source of truth
+
+- Drift/SQLite is the device source of truth for learning content, sessions,
+  SRS state, progress events, preferences required offline, and the sync
+  outbox.
+- Firebase Auth is the source of truth for account identity.
+- Firestore and trusted Firebase Functions are the synchronized source of
+  truth for registered-user cloud state, server-authoritative economy
+  operations, shared content, and recovery across devices.
+- The local database remains usable when remote services are unavailable.
+  Remote snapshots update local records; UI reads local projections rather
+  than waiting on the network.
+- SharedPreferences is limited to tiny non-relational boot preferences. The
+  existing progress store is migrated to Drift before v0.1.
+
+### 4.3 Offline-first sync
+
+Every syncable mutation has:
+
+- a cryptographically unique operation ID;
+- owner ID, entity type, entity ID, local revision, timestamp, and payload;
+- an outbox state of pending, in-flight, acknowledged, or failed;
+- bounded exponential backoff with jitter;
+- an idempotent remote write contract;
+- a retry classification that separates connection failures, authentication
+  failures, rejected data, and permanent conflicts.
+
+The sync worker starts after authenticated boot, after connectivity returns,
+and on a bounded background schedule. It never blocks saving a completed local
+learning session.
+
+Conflict rules are explicit:
+
+- append-only learning events merge by unique event ID;
+- SRS state derives from ordered immutable review events;
+- user-edited vocabulary uses latest accepted revision with a visible conflict
+  copy when both sides changed;
+- profile text uses server revision checks;
+- purchases, point credits, ownership, and achievement awards are processed
+  only by trusted server writers and are idempotent by operation ID;
+- deletions use tombstones until remote acknowledgement.
+
+Guest data stays local. On account upgrade, local records are assigned to the
+new Firebase UID and uploaded once through the same outbox. Signing out does
+not silently delete local data; the UI offers an explicit device-data removal
+action.
+
+### 4.4 On-device execution and device tiers
+
+The supported baseline is Android 10 or newer, 4 GB RAM, and 2 GB of available
+storage during installation and model setup. Devices with 6 GB RAM or more are
+the recommended experience. These thresholds are verified against the weakest
+physical device in the invited group; the ability to run a graphically
+intensive game is useful evidence but is not treated as proof that a phone can
+run an unbounded language or speech model.
+
+The application assigns a runtime capability tier using Android version,
+available memory, CPU architecture, model acceleration support, storage,
+thermal status, and a short bounded benchmark:
+
+- **Baseline tier:** all core learning, SRS, quizzes, game logic, Drift data,
+  native TTS, and a compact on-device camera model.
+- **Enhanced tier:** higher-frequency camera analysis, richer pronunciation
+  features, and additional offline content/model packs.
+- **Cloud-enhanced tier:** Gemini generation and OmniVoice when online and
+  explicitly configured.
+
+Feature meaning remains consistent across tiers. A lower tier may reduce model
+size, analysis frequency, animation complexity, or audio enhancement, but it
+must not fabricate a result or silently replace a requested feature with an
+unrelated one.
+
+Optional model packs:
+
+- download only after user consent, with Wi-Fi preference and visible size;
+- support pause, resume, retry, delete, and storage-space checks;
+- verify signed metadata and a cryptographic checksum before activation;
+- keep the previous valid version until the new pack is verified;
+- use versioned compatibility metadata and atomic activation;
+- never contain an API key, personal learning history, or undocumented
+  executable payload.
+
+Camera and speech inference is throttled according to lifecycle, temperature,
+and battery state. Heavy inference pauses when the app is backgrounded or the
+device reaches a severe thermal state. Models and audio buffers are loaded
+lazily and released under memory pressure. The baseline device must complete a
+30-minute representative learning session without an out-of-memory crash,
+uncontrolled background work, or sustained severe thermal state.
+
+## 5. Account design
+
+Firebase Auth remains the single identity provider in v0.1.
+
+### 5.1 Registration and verification
+
+- Validate normalized email, password policy, confirmation, and profile
+  fields before network submission.
+- Create the account, create an idempotent profile, send verification email,
+  and enter a verification-pending screen.
+- Unverified accounts may access only the explicitly allowed verification and
+  local recovery UI. They cannot write registered-user cloud data.
+- Resend is throttled in the UI and by provider limits. The screen supports
+  refresh/reload and resumes correctly after app restart.
+
+### 5.2 Sign-in and guest mode
+
+- Sign-in maps provider errors to stable Thai messages without leaking
+  implementation details.
+- Guest mode uses Firebase anonymous authentication when online and a local
+  guest session when initialization is temporarily unavailable. Cloud-private
+  data remains inaccessible until a verified account exists.
+- Upgrading an anonymous identity links credentials when possible so the UID
+  is retained. If the provider requires a new UID, migration is explicit and
+  idempotent.
+
+### 5.3 Forgot, reset, and change password
+
+- The login screen includes “ลืมรหัสผ่าน”.
+- Forgot-password accepts an email and always returns the same neutral
+  confirmation message, regardless of whether an account exists.
+- Firebase password reset links use the registered Android application link.
+  The app validates the action code before showing the new-password form.
+- The reset form validates password policy and confirmation, applies the
+  action code once, invalidates the local recovery state, and routes to
+  sign-in.
+- Change-password is available from profile settings. It requires a recently
+  authenticated user; stale sessions prompt for the current password and
+  reauthenticate before updating.
+- Offline requests do not pretend to succeed. Entered email may be preserved
+  locally for retry, but the app clearly asks the user to reconnect before the
+  recovery email can be sent.
+- Authentication attempts and recovery resend actions use cooldowns and
+  Firebase protections. Logs never contain email addresses, passwords, action
+  codes, ID tokens, or API keys.
+
+## 6. Gemini BYOK design
+
+Gemini is the sole AI provider exposed in v0.1.
+
+- A user enters their own Gemini API key in Settings after seeing a short
+  privacy and billing explanation.
+- The key is stored only in Android Keystore-backed encrypted storage. It is
+  never stored in Drift, SharedPreferences, Firestore, analytics, crash
+  reports, exports, source control, or build defines.
+- AI requests are made through a Gemini adapter whose logs redact headers,
+  query parameters, request bodies that may contain user content, and every
+  key-like value.
+- Settings provides validate, replace, and delete actions. Validation makes a
+  minimal request and distinguishes invalid key, quota exceeded, unsupported
+  model, connection failure, and service outage.
+- The production APK contains no developer Gemini key and no fallback secret.
+- Features with a curated offline equivalent use that equivalent when Gemini
+  is unavailable and label the source accurately. Features whose purpose is
+  live generative AI show an actionable unavailable state; they never invent a
+  response.
+- Gemini is not bundled or emulated by a large local language model. This
+  avoids excessive APK size, memory pressure, battery drain, and misleading
+  quality claims on baseline devices.
+- Prompts use bounded input/output length, age-appropriate system rules, and
+  response validation before generated content is added to a learning
+  session.
+
+## 7. Cloud and budget architecture
+
+### 7.1 Service selection
+
+- Firebase provides Auth, Firestore, App Check, trusted callable functions,
+  and operational telemetry.
+- A scale-to-zero managed container hosts OmniVoice only if the native TTS
+  fallback does not meet the requested voice mode. The client authenticates
+  with Firebase ID tokens; the service verifies identity and App Check.
+- Cloud services enhance the on-device product; they are not in the critical
+  path for opening the app, accessing downloaded learning content, completing
+  practice, or saving progress.
+- Supabase is removed from the v0.1 runtime because the current application
+  uses it only to generate public image URLs while storing structured data in
+  Firestore. Shop images move to packaged assets for the first 30 users or to
+  Firebase Storage if remote updates are required. This removes a second
+  identity/policy surface and the current publishable-key APK blocker.
+
+### 7.2 Cost controls
+
+The initial operational budget is capped at **THB 1,000 per month** until the
+owner changes it deliberately. Because cloud billing alerts alone are not a
+hard stop, enforcement combines:
+
+- billing alerts at 50%, 80%, and 100%;
+- per-user and per-device function rate limits;
+- maximum request and response sizes;
+- Cloud Run scale-to-zero with maximum one instance for the invited test;
+- daily project quotas for nonessential cloud actions;
+- a server-side kill switch for OmniVoice and other discretionary workloads;
+- Gemini costs charged to each user's own key and project, not LexiQuest;
+- a documented emergency action that disables cloud enhancements while
+  preserving offline learning.
+
+The invited-user gate and quotas are tested for 30 users before release. No
+paid production resource is deployed until its billing account, alerts,
+quotas, owner, and shutdown procedure are recorded.
+
+## 8. Feature completion rules
+
+### 8.1 Vocabulary, reading, and practice
+
+- The shared vocabulary seed and offline reading packs are versioned
+  production data, not demo records.
+- Personal categories and words support create, edit, delete, import, and
+  recovery after restart.
+- Every assessment draws distractors from valid vocabulary records and records
+  a stable completed-session event exactly once.
+- SRS, adaptive scheduling, weakness analysis, CEFR placement, associative
+  reading, and recommendations use persisted learner events rather than
+  screen-local sample lists.
+- Empty libraries show onboarding actions; they do not silently populate fake
+  user history.
+
+### 8.2 Voice and pronunciation
+
+- Native device TTS is the dependable offline baseline.
+- OmniVoice is an online enhancement with authentication, timeout,
+  cancellation, cache bounds, and truthful fallback metadata.
+- Speech recognition and pronunciation screens check microphone permission,
+  recognizer availability, language support, and offline capability.
+- Pronunciation feedback must be derived from real captured speech and the
+  documented evaluator. Heuristic-only scores are labeled as estimates and
+  cannot be presented as acoustic model measurements.
+- Audio playback and recording release resources on navigation and app
+  lifecycle changes.
+
+### 8.3 Object scanner
+
+The current vocabulary simulation is removed from production. Object scanner
+uses:
+
+- real camera preview and capture;
+- runtime camera permission handling;
+- on-device image labeling or an explicitly configured production model;
+- confidence thresholds and an “ไม่แน่ใจ” state;
+- user confirmation before saving a recognized word;
+- no captured-image upload by default.
+
+Unsupported devices receive a clear unavailable explanation and a manual
+vocabulary entry path. A random vocabulary result is never shown as a scan.
+Camera frame analysis uses the capability tier to select model size and
+frequency without changing the confirmation and confidence rules.
+
+### 8.4 Progress, world, achievements, and economy
+
+- All progress derives from idempotent completed-session events.
+- World nodes, bosses, quests, streaks, ranks, and achievements consume one
+  canonical progress projection and persist their awarded operation IDs.
+- Server-authoritative functions validate point credit, unlock, purchase, and
+  equipment operations. The Android client cannot mint spendable points,
+  debit balances, or create ownership documents directly.
+- Purchases atomically verify balance, prevent duplicates, write ownership,
+  and debit the canonical balance.
+- The shop, avatar equipment, wallpapers, and related navigation open only
+  after the trusted writer and migration pass. They are not permanently
+  hidden to make the APK pass.
+- Existing divergent legacy counters are migrated once and retained only for
+  compatibility/audit; they are not used as the v0.1 authority.
+
+### 8.5 Research and exports
+
+- Exports operate on the signed-in user's persisted records or an explicitly
+  selected local guest dataset.
+- Sample export cards and fabricated observations are removed from production
+  screens.
+- Every export includes schema version, time zone, app version, build ID,
+  applied filters, and whether records were local-only or synchronized.
+- Missing data yields a valid empty report with explanation, not invented
+  statistics.
+- File creation, storage permission, share-sheet cancellation, and partial
+  failures have tested user-visible outcomes.
+
+## 9. Navigation and unified UI
+
+### 9.1 Navigation
+
+A typed route registry replaces scattered direct `MaterialPageRoute` calls
+and the legacy drawer bridge.
+
+The canonical five top-level destinations remain:
+
+1. Learn
+2. Progress
+3. Weakness
+4. Rewards
+5. Profile
+
+Every included feature has exactly one production entry point under one of
+these destinations. Account recovery action links and notifications use the
+same route registry. Back behavior, process restoration, authentication
+guards, verification guards, guest restrictions, and deep links are tested.
+Duplicate screens are removed only after unique behavior and data paths have
+been migrated.
+
+### 9.2 Human design direction
+
+The UI uses one central Material 3 theme and component library:
+
+- calm blue/indigo primary palette with restrained semantic accents;
+- accessible surface contrast and typography with Thai/Latin metrics tested;
+- an 8-point spacing grid, consistent 12/16/24 corner families, and minimal
+  elevation;
+- one button hierarchy, form style, card style, dialog style, empty state,
+  loading state, and error state;
+- meaningful icons with text labels where ambiguity is possible;
+- motion only for feedback or spatial continuity;
+- no decorative gradient overload, emoji-heavy headings, generic AI sparkle
+  treatment, excessive glass effects, or inconsistent autogenerated copy.
+
+User-facing Thai copy is reviewed for natural wording and UTF-8 integrity.
+Old and new visual systems are not mixed in the same APK. Screenshots across
+the complete route inventory are part of the visual regression review.
+
+## 10. Error handling and observability
+
+- Domain errors map to stable user messages and retry actions.
+- Offline, timeout, unauthorized, quota, permission, validation, conflict, and
+  server errors remain distinguishable.
+- Global error boundaries prevent a single feature from crashing the shell.
+- Logs use structured event names and correlation IDs while excluding direct
+  personal identifiers, tokens, passwords, Gemini keys, action codes, raw
+  recordings, and vocabulary content unless the user explicitly exports it.
+- Health UI reports whether local learning, sync, Gemini, voice enhancement,
+  and cloud services are available. “Ready” is never shown when required
+  configuration is missing.
+- Crash and performance monitoring are opt-in for the invited test where
+  legally required, with retention and deletion behavior documented.
+
+## 11. Migration plan
+
+On first v0.1 launch:
+
+1. Back up the current local schema version marker.
+2. Migrate valid user vocabulary, categories, SRS records, progress sessions,
+   and selected preferences into the canonical Drift schema.
+3. Deduplicate records by stable IDs and documented normalized keys.
+4. Convert pending legacy progress into idempotent outbox events without
+   treating legacy client counters as authoritative currency.
+5. Preserve data that cannot be converted in a local quarantine table and
+   offer a diagnostic export.
+6. Commit the new schema marker only after the migration transaction
+   succeeds.
+
+Migration is restart-safe and rollback-tested from representative old
+databases. Uninstalling the old APK is not an acceptable migration strategy.
+
+## 12. Test strategy
+
+Implementation follows test-driven development: add a failing behavior test,
+implement the smallest production change, and refactor after it passes.
+
+Required coverage includes:
+
+- unit tests for validation, learning algorithms, sync state transitions,
+  conflict policy, rate limits, and economy invariants;
+- repository tests against real Drift databases;
+- Firebase emulator tests for Auth-adjacent flows, Firestore rules, trusted
+  progress writers, purchases, and idempotency;
+- adapter contract tests for Gemini, voice, camera/ML, speech, file export, and
+  platform permissions;
+- device-tier tests for capability detection, model selection, pack integrity,
+  low storage, memory pressure, battery saver, and thermal throttling;
+- widget tests for every screen state: loading, data, empty, offline,
+  recoverable error, and permanent unavailable;
+- navigation tests proving every inventory item is reachable and guarded;
+- integration tests for registration/verification, login, forgot/reset/change
+  password, guest upgrade, learning completion, offline restart, reconnect
+  sync, shop purchase, AI key lifecycle, scanner, voice fallback, and exports;
+- migration tests from captured historical schema fixtures;
+- accessibility checks for text scaling, touch targets, contrast, screen
+  reader labels, and keyboard/form order;
+- bounded dependency, secret, and policy audits using repository-approved
+  local tools;
+- manual exploratory testing on at least three representative physical
+  Android devices, including one Android 10/4 GB baseline device, followed by
+  the 30-person invited acceptance test.
+
+Production-code scans fail when they find known mock/sample/simulation entry
+points, embedded secrets, obsolete fixed tunnel URLs, or unguarded direct
+remote writes. Test fakes remain permitted under test-only paths.
+
+## 13. Release gates
+
+The exact release commit must satisfy all of the following:
+
+1. `flutter analyze` passes with no errors or warnings accepted as release
+   exceptions.
+2. All Flutter unit, widget, integration, backend, rules, and function tests
+   pass.
+3. The complete CLI verification suite passes from a clean checkout.
+4. Android release configuration uses HTTPS, production service identifiers,
+   enforced App Check, least-privilege policies, and no secret in the APK.
+5. A signed `flutter build apk --release` succeeds with version `0.1.0+1`.
+6. The APK installs, launches, upgrades from the prior test build, survives
+   restart, and completes every critical journey on physical devices.
+7. Offline learning, queued sync, reconnect, duplicate delivery, conflict,
+   and interrupted migration scenarios pass.
+8. All production routes are reachable; no production control returns sample,
+   simulated, placeholder, or fabricated data.
+9. UI review confirms one design system and natural Thai copy across the full
+   screenshot inventory.
+10. The Android 10/4 GB baseline device completes the representative
+    30-minute session without an out-of-memory crash or sustained severe
+    thermal state.
+11. Optional model downloads pass consent, storage, resume, checksum,
+    compatibility, rollback, and deletion tests.
+12. Billing alerts, enforceable quotas, runtime kill switches, rollback
+    instructions, and service ownership are verified.
+13. The invited test consent, privacy notice, support channel, known external
+    dependency limitations, and feedback path are ready.
+14. The final APK checksum, commit SHA, build command, dependency lockfiles,
+    migration version, test evidence, and rollback artifact are recorded.
+
+No APK is distributed if any gate fails. A debug APK, a build produced with
+missing runtime configuration, or a build whose unavailable features are
+disguised as success is not a v0.1 candidate.
+
+## 14. Current baseline findings
+
+The pre-implementation verification on the isolated release worktree found:
+
+- runtime probes, Android command tests, contract checks, Flutter analysis,
+  709 Flutter tests, trusted-writer tests, Firestore emulator tests, voice API
+  tests, AI API tests, and language-model tests passed;
+- the Android debug APK phase failed because
+  `LEXIQUEST_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` was absent/invalid;
+- the root JavaScript dependency audit reported five high-severity findings,
+  while the Functions dependency audit reported none;
+- production code still contains sample/simulation paths, including the object
+  scanner, export center, ghost duel, tutor fallback, and story content;
+- the current app declares `1.0.0+1`, mixes theme definitions, and still uses a
+  legacy navigation bridge.
+
+These are release blockers to be closed, not accepted exceptions. Removing the
+unnecessary Supabase runtime dependency resolves the current build
+configuration blocker as part of the approved architecture; dependency
+findings are handled through bounded upgrade and regression testing rather
+than an unreviewed automatic rewrite.
+
+## 15. Definition of done
+
+Work is complete only when:
+
+- every included feature satisfies its behavior and failure-state acceptance
+  tests;
+- accounts include working forgot/reset/change-password flows;
+- core learning is functional offline and synchronization is proven after
+  reconnect;
+- the on-device baseline works on Android 10/4 GB and scales enhanced models
+  only when device capability permits;
+- Gemini BYOK is secure, replaceable, removable, and the only AI provider;
+- production contains no reachable fabricated data or simulated device
+  capability;
+- the economy and ownership paths use trusted idempotent writers;
+- UI, navigation, and Thai copy are consistent across the application;
+- migration preserves existing valid user data;
+- the signed release APK passes all gates and the invited 30-user rollout has
+  a rollback path;
+- the historical comparison remains available through `baseline/2024-10`.
